  "question": {
    "question_id": "gold-mtor-vaccin",
    "sous_question": "Chez l'humain âgé, un inhibiteur de mTOR (rapamycine ou analogue) augmente-t-il la réponse en anticorps au vaccin antigrippal ?",
    "espece_cible": "human_in_vivo",
    "population": "adultes de 65 ans et plus, vivant à domicile ou en institution",
    "intervention": "inhibiteur de mTOR (rapamycine, évérolimus/RAD001, BEZ235) à faible dose contre placebo, avant vaccination",
    "mesurande": {
      "nom": "réponse en anticorps au vaccin antigrippal",
      "unite": "ratio de titres"
    },
    "exclusions": [],
    "requetes_initiales": {
      "pubmed": [
        "mTOR inhibitor influenza vaccine elderly"
      ],
      "arxiv": []
    }
  },
  "citation_verbatim": "RAD001 enhanced the response to the influenza vaccine by about 20% at doses that were relatively well tolerated.",
  "source_id": "pmid:25540326",
  "drapeaux": {
    "is_future_or_protocol": false,
    "species_hits": [],
    "human_hits": []
  },
  "contextes": [
    {
      "indice": 0,
      "type": "document_entier",
      "texte": "[TITRE] mTOR inhibition improves immune function in the elderly.\n\n[RÉSUMÉ]\n\nInhibition of the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) pathway extends life span in all species studied to date, and in mice delays the onset of age-related diseases and comorbidities. However, it is unknown if mTOR inhibition affects aging or its consequences in humans. To begin to assess the effects of mTOR inhibition on human aging-related conditions, we evaluated whether the mTOR inhibitor RAD001 ameliorated immunosenescence (the decline in immune function during aging) in elderly volunteers, as assessed by their response to influenza vaccination. <<<RAD001 enhanced the response to the influenza vaccine by about 20% at doses that were relatively well tolerated.>>> RAD001 also reduced the percentage of CD4 and CD8 T lymphocytes expressing the programmed death-1 (PD-1) receptor, which inhibits T cell signaling and is more highly expressed with age. These results raise the possibility that mTOR inhibition may have beneficial effects on immunosenescence in the elderly."
    }
  ],
